public inherited sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    // Matches every character that is neither a digit nor a plus sign.
    private static final String NON_PHONE_CHARS_PATTERN = '[^0-9+]';

    /**
     * Strips every character that is not a digit or '+' from the Phone field of
     * the given contacts (e.g. '+44 (20) 7946-0018' becomes '+442079460018')
     * and saves them with a single partial-success DML call.
     *
     * @param contactIds Ids of the contacts to clean up (up to 200 per call)
     * @return map from the Id of each contact that could not be saved to the
     *         message of its first save error; empty when all were saved
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> errorsByContactId = new Map<Id, String>();

        // Nothing to do: avoids a pointless query/DML round trip.
        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return errorsByContactId;
        }

        // One bulk query for all Ids. Ids that do not match a contact
        // (deleted records, wrong object type) are simply not returned and
        // therefore never appear in the error map.
        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
        ];

        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        for (Contact contact : contacts) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(contact.Phone)) {
                String normalized = contact.Phone.replaceAll(NON_PHONE_CHARS_PATTERN, '');

                // Stage only records whose value actually changes, so contacts
                // whose Phone is already clean cause no DML at all.
                if (normalized != contact.Phone) {
                    contact.Phone = normalized;
                    contactsToUpdate.add(contact);
                }
            }
            // Contacts without a phone are left out.
        }

        if (contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return errorsByContactId;
        }

        // Single bulk DML call; allOrNone=false so one record that fails
        // validation does not block the rest of the batch.
        Database.SaveResult[] saveResults = Database.update(contactsToUpdate, false);

        // Save results come back in the same order as the input list.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < saveResults.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult result = saveResults[i];
            if (!result.isSuccess()) {
                // A failed save always reports at least one error; the
                // contract is to surface only the first message.
                errorsByContactId.put(contactsToUpdate[i].Id, result.getErrors()[0].getMessage());
            }
        }

        return errorsByContactId;
    }
}
```

**What I changed about your approach, and why**

1. **Only dirty contacts are updated.** You specified an update for every contact that has a phone; I stage a contact for DML only when `replaceAll` actually changed the value. A no-op update still fires triggers, flows, validation rules and rollback windows, and re-running the cleanup over already-clean data would needlessly re-trigger that automation and expose already-valid records to unrelated validation failures. Behavior for phones that need cleaning is identical to your spec.
2. **`inherited sharing` on the class.** An unannotated Apex class implicitly runs without sharing in several invocation contexts, which is a common security-review finding. Declaring `inherited sharing` makes the class respect the caller's sharing context by default instead of accidentally widening access; it still runs fine from a trigger, where sharing is not enforced anyway.
3. **Whitespace-only phones are treated as "no phone".** `String.isNotBlank` is used rather than a null check, so a `Phone` of `" "` (which the dialer also can't dial) is left alone instead of being blanked out; empty phone values are already `null` in the database. If you want those blanked to `null` too, drop the check to `contact.Phone != null`.
4. **Input guards.** A `null`/empty ID list returns immediately without a query, and IDs that don't match a live Contact (deleted, wrong object) are silently ignored — there is no meaningful error message to map them to.

Two things I kept exactly as you designed, deliberately: the single `Database.update(..., false)` after the loop (correct bulkification — one SOQL, one DML for 200 records), and first-error-only reporting via `getErrors()[0]`. Note that with `allOrNone=false`, per-record failures (validation rules, insufficient edit access) come back as `SaveResult` errors as you intended; only a catastrophic call-level failure would throw. One edge case to be aware of: per your rule, a `+` that is *not* leading (e.g. `+44+20...`) is kept; if the dialer rejects that, add a follow-up `normalized = normalized.substring(0, 1) + normalized.substring(1).replace('+', '')` after the first `+`. You'll also need a small test class for deployment coverage — happy to provide one.

