/**
 * AccountCountrySync
 *
 * Called from AccountTrigger (after update).
 *
 * When an Account's BillingCountry changes, every Contact of that account
 * whose MailingCountry is blank or still equals the Account's previous
 * BillingCountry "follows the headquarters": its MailingCountry is set to the
 * new BillingCountry. Contacts with a different MailingCountry (people working
 * abroad) and contacts of accounts whose BillingCountry did not change are
 * left untouched.
 *
 * Bulk design (MDM pushes accounts in 200-record batches, ~2.3M contacts):
 *  - Exactly one selective SOQL per transaction, no SOQL or DML inside loops.
 *    The query is filtered to the changed accounts and to candidate mailing
 *    countries, so it never loads the Contact table.
 *  - Synchronous DML is capped below the 10,000 DML-rows-per-transaction
 *    limit. Anything larger is handed to the Queueable implementation at the
 *    bottom of this class, which updates in 10,000-row chunks and chains
 *    itself until the backlog is drained.
 *
 * `inherited sharing`: runs in the caller's sharing mode; from the trigger /
 * MDM context this executes in system mode, which is intended for this
 * automation.
 */
public inherited sharing class AccountCountrySync implements Queueable {

    /**
     * Contacts to update above this count are processed asynchronously, so a
     * large MDM batch can never break the 10,000 DML-rows-per-transaction
     * limit. Kept at 9,000 to leave headroom for other DML in the same
     * transaction. Non-final and @TestVisible so tests can lower the cap
     * without seeding thousands of contacts.
     */
    @TestVisible
    private static Integer syncDmlRowCap = 9000;

    /** Rows updated per Queueable execution (async limit is 10,000 DML rows). */
    private static final Integer ASYNC_CHUNK_SIZE = 10000;

    /**
     * Trigger entry point.
     *
     * @param newAccounts Trigger.new (after update)
     * @param oldMap      Trigger.oldMap with pre-update field values
     */
    public static void syncBillingCountryToContacts(List<Account> newAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldMap) {
        if (newAccounts == null || newAccounts.isEmpty() || oldMap == null) {
            return;
        }

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1. Isolate the accounts whose BillingCountry actually changed.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        Map<Id, String> newCountryByAccountId = new Map<Id, String>();
        Map<Id, String> oldCountryByAccountId = new Map<Id, String>();
        Set<String> oldCountries = new Set<String>();

        for (Account acc : newAccounts) {
            Account before = oldMap.get(acc.Id);
            String oldCountry = (before == null) ? null : before.BillingCountry;
            String newCountry = acc.BillingCountry;

            // Apex String == is a null-safe value comparison, so this also
            // treats blank -> blank as "no change".
            if (newCountry == oldCountry) {
                continue; // country unchanged: this account's contacts stay alone
            }

            newCountryByAccountId.put(acc.Id, newCountry);
            oldCountryByAccountId.put(acc.Id, oldCountry);
            if (oldCountry != null) {
                oldCountries.add(oldCountry);
            }
        }

        if (newCountryByAccountId.isEmpty()) {
            return; // nothing changed in this batch
        }

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2. Single selective query per transaction (never per record, and
        //    never the whole Contact table - at ~2.3M contacts that would
        //    hit the 50,000 query-rows limit and the heap limit).
        //
        //    Fetch only contacts of changed accounts whose MailingCountry is
        //    blank or matches *some* changed account's previous country. The
        //    exact per-account old/new pairing is re-checked in memory below,
        //    so a contact is never updated because of another account's old
        //    country.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        List<Contact> candidates = [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :newCountryByAccountId.keySet()
              AND (MailingCountry = NULL OR MailingCountry IN :oldCountries)
        ];

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3. Keep only the true "followers" and build partial sObjects so the
        //    DML writes nothing but MailingCountry.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : candidates) {
            String oldCountry = oldCountryByAccountId.get(c.AccountId);
            Boolean isFollower =
                c.MailingCountry == null
                || (oldCountry != null && oldCountry == c.MailingCountry);

            if (isFollower) {
                toUpdate.add(new Contact(
                    Id = c.Id,
                    MailingCountry = newCountryByAccountId.get(c.AccountId)
                ));
            }
        }

        if (toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 4. One DML call, outside all loops. Small volumes run synchronously
        //    in the same transaction; large volumes are deferred to the
        //    Queueable below so the 10,000 DML-rows limit is never exceeded.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        if (toUpdate.size() <= syncDmlRowCap) {
            update toUpdate;
        } else {
            System.enqueueJob(new AccountCountrySync(toUpdate));
        }
    }

    // ======================================================================
    // Asynchronous overflow path
    // ======================================================================

    /** State container for the deferred contacts; set via the constructor. */
    private final List<Contact> pendingContacts;

    private AccountCountrySync(List<Contact> pendingContacts) {
        this.pendingContacts = pendingContacts;
    }

    /**
     * Updates the deferred contacts in chunks of ASYNC_CHUNK_SIZE and chains
     * itself while a backlog remains. Each execution runs in its own
     * transaction with fresh governor limits, so even a 50,000-contact batch
     * (the most a single SOQL can return) finishes in a handful of queued
     * jobs. Any failure fails the job visibly rather than partially
     * committing silently.
     */
    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (pendingContacts == null || pendingContacts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Integer chunkEnd = Math.min(ASYNC_CHUNK_SIZE, pendingContacts.size());
        update new List<Contact>(pendingContacts.subList(0, chunkEnd));

        if (chunkEnd < pendingContacts.size()) {
            System.enqueueJob(new AccountCountrySync(
                new List<Contact>(pendingContacts.subList(chunkEnd, pendingContacts.size()))
            ));
        }
    }
}
```

## What I changed from your approach, and why

**1. I replaced "load all contacts into a map" with one *selective* query — but kept the "one query, no loops" contract.**
`SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact` against 2.3M contacts throws an uncatchable `System.LimitException: Too many query rows: 50001` (per-transaction SOQL row limit is 50,000) and would exhaust the 6 MB heap long before that. Every MDM batch of 200 would die. The handler still issues exactly **one SOQL per transaction**, no queries in loops — it just pushes the filtering into the database: `WHERE AccountId IN :changedIds AND (MailingCountry = NULL OR MailingCountry IN :oldCountries)`. This also automatically excludes contacts of accounts whose `BillingCountry` didn't change, which the full-table map approach would have required extra logic to exclude. Because `MailingCountry IN :oldCountries` can over-fetch (a contact of account A whose country coincidentally equals account B's *old* country), the handler re-checks each contact against **its own** account's previous value in memory — so "working abroad" contacts are still never touched.

**2. I added an async overflow path for the 10,000 DML-rows-per-transaction limit.**
This is the second bulk-safety problem at your scale: a 200-account batch × the matching contacts on those accounts can easily exceed 10,000 contact updates in one transaction, which would roll back the entire MDM batch. The handler updates up to 9,000 contacts synchronously (immediate, same transaction) and defers anything larger to a chained Queueable (at the bottom of `AccountCountrySync`, since the file list was fixed) that processes 10,000 rows per transaction until done. If you know your 200-account batches can never produce more than ~9,000 matching contacts, you can delete the async section and keep only the inline `update`.

**3. Smaller details worth knowing:**
- Null-safe `==` handles all transitions: blank→value, value→blank (contacts get blanked along with the billing country), null→value.
- DML uses partial sObjects (`Id` + `MailingCountry` only), so nothing else on the contact is written, and it's a single `update` statement outside all loops.
- Trade-off: for oversized batches, the contact updates land a few seconds later in separate transactions rather than atomically with the account update. If strict same-transaction consistency for huge batches is a hard requirement, the alternative is handing the changed account Ids to Batch Apex instead — same logic, different delivery — but for keeping contacts in step with an MDM feed, the Queueable fallback is the pragmatic choice.

